<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Germania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Germania was the Roman name for the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> never permanently conquered after its defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> in 9 CE. The name was also given to the Roman provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> and Germania Superior on the left bank of the Rhine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> described its peoples in his ethnography Germania of 98 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/germania.jpg" alt="Germania" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Lancaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germanias.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Roman geographers placed Germania roughly between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in the east, from the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> northwards along the North and Baltic seas to the known parts of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. The free lands are also called Magna Germania, and by modern scholars Germania Libera or the Germanic Barbaricum. The origin of the name Germani is uncertain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> wrote that the name had once belonged only to the Tungri, west of the Rhine, and was later extended to supposedly related peoples beyond it.</p><p>The country held many peoples with little unity among them, and river names suggest that another Indo-European group lived there, probably under Germanic domination. Archaeologically it corresponds, from the 1st to the 4th century CE, to the Roman Iron Age: largely agrarian, without cities or coinage of its own. Amber was a primary export, and Roman luxury goods were imported on a large scale and have been found as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> met peoples from beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> and called their homeland Germania, a name the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> appear to have borrowed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>. Augustus later carried Roman arms as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and in 7 BCE created a short-lived province, Germania Antiqua, before the victory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> ended the attempt. The Rhine became the frontier. Germani such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubii">Ubii</a> and Sicambri were settled on the Roman bank, which was heavily affected by the Revolt of the Batavi under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-civilis">Gaius Julius Civilis</a> in 69–70 CE.</p><p>Under the Flavian dynasty the two provinces were organised, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> with its headquarters at Colonia, the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, and Germania Superior at Mogontiacum. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> also occupied the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, Main and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agri-decumates">Agri Decumates</a>. In the 3rd century confederations such as the Alemanni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> emerged and raided the provinces, and around 280 CE the Agri Decumates were evacuated and taken by the Alemanni. In 406 a large number of people fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> crossed the Rhine, and Roman rule there eventually collapsed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Much of Germania, including all the former Roman provinces, was eventually incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish Empire</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>. The name survived in learned use: it is attested in Old English translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orosius">Orosius</a>, and since the 17th century the usual English name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> has been derived from it, as are the names of that country in many other languages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Germania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Roger Beck lists the places of the earliest phase (c. 80–120 CE): Rome, Nida and Mogontiacum in <b>Germania</b>, Pons Aeni in Noricum, Carnuntum in Pannonia, Novae and Oescus in Moesia, and Caesarea Maritima in Judaea.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>He besieged Cologne and killed Gallienus' son Saloninus there and won the loyalty of Gaul, <b>Germania</b>, Britain and Hispania.</blockquote><a href="/state/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Caesar describes the Suebi as the largest and most warlike people of <b>Germania</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a></li></ul></section>
